# Every function the reference ownership checker (bin/rig-oracle) and the
# compiler's own decide differently, one a line:
#
#   <program> <function> prod-rejects <class> <note>
#
# Only prod-rejects differences are listed: the compiler rejects what
# the oracle accepts. A prod-accepts difference (the compiler accepts
# what the oracle rejects) always fails the run and cannot be listed.
# Classes: prod-strict (the compiler rejects what the Core allows),
# ref-gap (the oracle is wrong or less precise), core-gap (docs/CORE.md
# does not decide it). A program is its test path, a doc
# example's id (doc/CORE/L120), or a matrix cell (vec.print.place).

# A `for x in ?v` element is a read view; a bare read view copies (Core
# s1), so binding it inside the loop, or returning one that views only
# what the function was lent (s7), keeps every loan in order.
test/corpus/review-box-p117.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict `y = x` of a `for x in ?v` element view
test/corpus/review-box-p142.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict `new x = x` of a `for x in ?v` element view
test/corpus/syntax-review-complete-g7.rig Map.find prod-rejects prod-strict returns a `for e in ?self.es` element view of the receiver

# Whether writing through a fixed `w =! !n` (`w = 5`, `w += 1`) reassigns
# `w`: Core §6 forbids reassigning a const binding, and SPEC §7 says a
# write view's assignment writes through.
test/corpus/review-lang4-fx.rig main prod-rejects core-gap `w += 1` through a fixed write view
test/corpus/review-place-b9-x022_fixed_wb_local.rig main prod-rejects core-gap `w = 5` through a fixed write view

# Whether a `match !e` payload binding, a write view of the payload, may
# be moved into a local (`y = <s`): SPEC §7 moves a write view with `<w`,
# and the compiler reads `<s` as moving out of `e`.
test/corpus/review-take-p15.rig main prod-rejects core-gap `y = <s` of a `match !e` payload binding

# `swap` and `replace` store a view in a borrowed parameter, so the
# stored view's loans join that parameter's flow and the owner stays lent;
# the oracle's model of those calls does not carry the stored loan yet.
store.text.replace.grow f prod-rejects ref-gap `replace` stores a String view of `b` in `a`, then `b` grows
store.text.swap.grow f prod-rejects ref-gap `swap` stores a String view of `b` in `a`, then `b` grows

# A `defer` runs at its scope's exits, after the values declared after
# it are dropped (Core §7, SPEC "defer and errdefer"). The compiler also
# checks deferred code where it is written, and its liveness counts
# every use in deferred code, so a view whose last use comes before the
# deferred code runs still holds its loan there.
test/corpus/r3-or-defer-strict.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict `defer !v.push(3)` before `w = !v`, whose last use precedes the exit
test/corpus/review-take-p53.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict deferred `<s.a` after `print(r)`, the last use of `r = ?s`
test/corpus/review-take-p54.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict deferred `<s.a` runs after the deferred `print(w)`, the last use of `w = !s`

# A `for k in ?v` element over a Vec of handles is a read view of the
# slot (Core §4); storing it in a binding declared before the loop keeps
# the loan on `v`, which `v` outlives (Core s6).
test/corpus/r3-review1-p33b_vec_store_opt.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict `saved = k` of a `for k in ?v` element view
test/corpus/review-step1-p33b_vec_store_opt.rig main prod-rejects prod-strict `saved = k` of a `for k in ?v` element view

# A generic body's copy of a `T` is checked per instance (SPEC "Generic
# bodies"); the compiler reports an owning instance inside the function
# that names it, which the oracle does not check.
test/corpus/audit-ownership-q02 main prod-rejects ref-gap `Holder[*Tok].peek` copies an owning `T`
